<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moskva River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moskva River is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 470 kilometres long, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Upland and flows east through Moscow to join the Oka at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a>. The city of Moscow grew on its banks from a small settlement first mentioned in 1147, and control of the river and its route towards the Oka and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> helped the early princes of Moscow extend their power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moskva-river.jpg" alt="Moskva River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basile Starynkevitch · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moskva_kremlin_04_2007.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The river rises about 140 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, crosses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and Moscow Oblasts and reaches the Oka about 110 kilometres south-east of the capital. Its length is given as 473 kilometres, or by another measure 502, its basin covers 17,600 square kilometres, and it falls 155 metres along its course. An estimated 61 per cent of its water comes from thaw, 12 per cent from rain and 27 per cent from underground sources. It normally freezes around November or December and begins to thaw in late March. On its banks, besides Moscow, stand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a>, Zvenigorod, Zhukovsky, Bronnitsy and Voskresensk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Recent studies suggest that the river has occupied its present bed for about 12,000 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> lived along it, as did Slavic tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyatichians">Vyatichi</a>. The name of the city is thought to come from that of the river. The most widely accepted explanation derives it from a Proto-Balto-Slavic root connected with wetness, so that it may have meant a river in a marsh or wetland. One Finno-Ugric hypothesis holds instead that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merya">Merya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muroma">Muroma</a>, pre-Slavic peoples of the area, called it Mustajoki, black river. To tell the two apart, Russians usually call the river Moskva-reka.</p><p>The first stone bridge across it, the Bolshoy Kamenny Bridge, was built in 1692. The Vodootvodny Canal, dug in the 1780s, created the island opposite the Kremlin that Muscovites informally call Bolotny Ostrov, Bog Island. Dams built in 1785, 1836 and 1878 failed to keep the river navigable through summer droughts. The highest recorded water level in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, 127.25 metres above sea level against a usual 120, was reached in the flood of 1908.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since the Moscow Canal was completed, in 1932–1937, the river has also carried a share of water from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, which has made commercial shipping reliable. The average discharge, Volga water included, rises from 38 cubic metres a second near Zvenigorod to 250 at the mouth. As of 2007 there were 49 bridges over the river and its canals within the city, where it is 120 to 200 metres wide and narrowest under the Kremlin walls. Moscow draws its drinking water from five stations on the river and from the upper Volga reservoirs. The stretch within the city freezes only occasionally, on account of contamination.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moskva (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moscow, a small fortified settlement on the <b>Moskva River</b> first mentioned in the chronicles under 1147, lay on the western edge of the Rus' principality of Vladimir-Suzdal, which had submitted to the Mongols after their invasion of 1237–1240.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Meine said the idea came to him one summer night as he sat at the Gorky Park centre looking at the <b>Moskva River</b>, and called the song his personal reappraisal of what had happened in the world in recent years.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wind-of-change">Wind of Change</a></li><li><blockquote>Mozhaysk, on the <b>Moskva</b> river west of Moscow, was taken from Smolensk in 1303.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-mozhaysk">Principality of Mozhaysk</a></li></ul></section>
